## 1. The noon forecast, and what v3 changes

- **Three clocks.** The forecast for day D (in standard time) is issued at its origin, D−1 at {{ K.origin_utc_hm }} UTC, that is {{ K.origin_local_hm }} EST, or {{ K.origin_local_dst_hm }} EDT under daylight time. D's hours do not move: each carries the UTC hour it ends at, from D at {{ K.first_label_hm }} UTC to the next day at {{ K.last_label_hm }} UTC. D is scored one hour after its last hour, as in v2. v2's form at midnight keeps v2's origin, D at {{ K.midnight_utc_hm }} UTC.
- **The inputs, known at the origin.** Demand {{ K.lags_noon[0] }} h and {{ K.lags_noon[1] }} h before the target hour (the demand {{ K.lags_midnight[0] }} h before is not known for half of D); the local hour and the day type; the NBM forecast temperature and wind at the target hour, from the run that feeds D; the mean of the forecast temperatures of D's 24 hours; the mean of ECCC's observed temperatures at the primary station over the 24 hours of D−2 (the lag day), which exists only with at least {{ K.lag_day_min_hours }} usable hours: otherwise it is missing, and nothing is imputed.
- **The availability assumption.** An NBM weather forecast (one run) is assumed available {{ K.availability_lag_h|num }} hours after its run time, and an ECCC hour from its own label, with its value as fetched: neither the publication delay nor later quality control is modelled. A validation variant makes each run available {{ K.sensitivity_lag_h|num }} hours after its run time (section 2.6).
- **The three terms**, all linear, each with base {{ K.base_c|num }} °C: the day's mean cold, max(0, {{ K.base_c|num }} − the forecast mean of D's hours); the lag day's mean cold, max(0, {{ K.base_c|num }} − D−2's observed mean), which lets the model take out of the demand {{ K.lags_noon[0] }} hours earlier the share that was D−2's own weather; wind with cold, the forecast wind in km/h (knots × {{ K.knots_to_kmh|num(3) }}) times max(0, {{ K.base_c|num }} − the hour's forecast temperature), zero in the warm. The forecast wind must lie between {{ K.wind_min_kt|num }} and {{ K.wind_max_kt|num }} knots, both included. No term reads demand.
- **The comparison models**, which never recommend anything: v2's form at noon (v2 exactly, on v3's rows, without the terms); v1's form at noon (v1's least squares on the same forecast); v2's form at midnight (v2 with all of its clocks and inputs, to measure what issuing twelve hours earlier costs).
- **The error.** The mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) says by how much, on average, the forecast misses actual demand, as a percentage of it. The signed bias says in which direction: bias = (demand − forecast) / demand, positive when the forecast is too low.
- **The interval, and its coverage.** Each forecast carries an interval, forecast × (1 ± q). The half-width q is the ⌈(n + 1) × {{ K.nominal|num(2) }}⌉-th smallest of the n relative errors |demand − forecast| / forecast of the {{ v.conformal.calibration_folds }} months before, already scored: the form of a split conformal interval, without its guarantee, since these hourly errors are autocorrelated. Coverage is the share of hours whose demand falls inside the interval; its target is {{ K.nominal|pct(0) }}. It is measured only from the first month preceded by {{ v.conformal.calibration_folds }} scored months, on fewer hours than the error.
- **Intervals by temperature.** That quantile is taken separately for the cold hours, below each fit's edge (the {{ (100 * K.bin_quantile)|num }}th percentile of the forecast temperatures of its own training rows), and for the rest. A cold bin of fewer than {{ K.min_hours }} errors, or of errors from fewer than {{ K.min_weeks }} Monday-to-Sunday weeks, takes the pooled half-width of all the errors, and the model records it.
- **The variance inflation factor** of a coefficient says how much its variance grows because its column overlaps the model's other columns: the larger it is, the less the coefficient reads on its own.
- **The decision rule.** On the common hours (the target and the three noon forecasts), the paired difference in hourly absolute percentage error, with a {{ K.ci_level|pct(0) }} week-block interval ({{ v.config.n_boot|num }} resamples in this validation; each replay gives its own, sections 3 and 4): **terms**, v3 minus v2's form at noon; **family**, v2's form at noon minus v1's form at noon; their sum, the whole change, reported and never judged; and the **horizon**, v2's form at noon minus its form at midnight, reported and never judged. A negative difference is a lower error: it is better when its interval's upper bound is below zero, worse when its lower bound is above it, not separated otherwise. The subsets: all common hours; the cold tail, those whose forecast temperature (noon issue) lies below the {{ (100 * K.bin_quantile)|num }}th percentile of the design years'; the event windows, those inside the rate's peak hours: December 1 to March 31, less December {{ and_list(K.excluded_december) }}, January {{ and_list(K.excluded_january) }}, Good Friday and Easter Monday (art. 2.67), the hours ending at {{ and_list(K.windows.morning) }} and at {{ and_list(K.windows.evening) }} o'clock, local time. A subset is not testable under {{ K.min_hours }} hours or {{ K.min_weeks }} weeks. **The terms verdict**: better when the event-window contrast is better, the all-hours one not worse and the cold tail testable and not worse; "better in the event windows, the cold tail not testable" when it is not testable; not shown better when the event-window contrast is not separated, or better but paid for elsewhere; worse when it is worse; not testable when the windows are not. **The family verdict** follows v2's rule: all common hours decide and the cold tail guards; better when the all-hours contrast is better and the cold tail, testable, is not worse; "better on average, the cold tail not testable" when it is not testable; not shown better when that contrast is not separated, or better with a worse cold tail; worse when it is worse. "The terms improve the noon forecast in the peak-event windows" is said only from the terms verdict, "the additive model earns its place over v1's form at noon" only from the family verdict, and both only on held-out data.
- **The window ranking.** In each scored period, each part of a winter is ranked on its own: its w eligible windows (four hours with a target and the three noon forecasts) are ranked by actual mean demand and by each noon model's mean forecast; k = floor({{ K.rate_events }} × w / W + 0.5), where {{ K.rate_events }} is the most events a winter holds ({{ K.winter_event_hours }} hours in events of {{ K.event_hours }} hours, art. 2.70) and W the windows of the whole winter, two a day outside the excluded days; for each model, how many of its k highest are among the k highest by actual demand. v2's form at midnight is not ranked: it is issued after the notice.

